  <div class="lh-container">
    

<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Birchas HaMazon U'Mayim Acharonim<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">Halachos 1–2</span></div>

<!-- HALACHAH 1 -->
<div class="sn" style="font-size:1.25rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);">Halachah 1</div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="lr">LM I:17</span> (<span class="v">"Vayhi Heim Merikim"</span>) and <span class="lr">LM I:47</span> (<span class="v">"Va'Achaltem Achol"</span>)</div>

<div class="p">As explained above in <span class="h">Hilchos Netilas Yadayim</span> and <span class="h">Betzi'as HaPas</span>, Halachah 1: eating is the aspect of drawing <span class="h">da'as</span> [holy awareness]. Rabbainu also writes in <span class="lr">LM I:17</span> that through eating, the <span class="h">da'as</span> is completed.</div>

<div class="sn">Mayim Acharonim: Sustaining the Da'as</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must wash <span class="h">mayim acharonim</span> [final waters] after eating, in order to give the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> its share, as is known — for its primary sustenance comes from the table/altar. One must give them sustenance in a measured way so they do not overpower and — Heaven forbid — destroy the <span class="h">da'as</span> entirely. <span class="h">Mayim acharonim</span> = the sustaining of the <span class="h">da'as</span>: without it, they would overpower.</div>

<div class="p">The Sages linked the two washings, calling them <span class="h">mayim rishonim</span> and <span class="h">mayim acharonim</span>, for both serve one purpose: <span class="h">mayim rishonim</span> purify the hands so as to <span class="kc">draw</span> the <span class="h">da'as</span>; <span class="h">mayim acharonim</span>, after the <span class="h">da'as</span> has been drawn through eating, give the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> its due to <span class="kc">sustain</span> the <span class="h">da'as</span>.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"You shall sanctify yourselves [<span class="h">v'hiskadishtem</span>]"</span> = <span class="h">mayim rishonim</span>; <span class="v">"and you shall be holy [<span class="h">vi'hyisem kedoshim</span>]"</span> = <span class="h">mayim acharonim</span>. Both are holiness, for the essence of holiness is the <span class="h">da'as</span>. "<span class="h">V'hiskadishtem</span>" = active sanctification, becoming holy anew = drawing <span class="h">da'as</span> through <span class="h">mayim rishonim</span>. "<span class="h">Vi'hyisem</span>" = a state of enduring holiness, ensuring it persists = <span class="h">mayim acharonim</span> that sustains the <span class="h">da'as</span>.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Mayim acharonim</span> may be performed with any liquid (not specifically water): <span class="h">mayim rishonim</span>, which draw the <span class="h">da'as</span>, require raising the hands and a <span class="h">berachah</span> — therefore they need actual water (= <span class="h">da'as</span>). But <span class="h">mayim acharonim</span> only need to remove contamination and give the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> its portion — no water specifically required, no raising of hands, no <span class="h">berachah</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">Birchas HaMazon: Connecting to the Tzadik</div>

<div class="p">Immediately after <span class="h">mayim acharonim</span> — having achieved the completion of the <span class="h">da'as</span> — one merits <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span>. Through the complete <span class="h">da'as</span>, one binds oneself to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (as in <span class="lr">LM I:17</span>). When bound to the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, all blessings flow: <span class="v">"blessings upon the head of the <span class="h">tzadik</span>."</span> This is <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span>: drawing blessings through the <span class="h">tzadik</span> by binding to him through eating = the completion of <span class="h">da'as</span>. The <span class="h">orach</span> [guest] blesses = <span class="v">"the path of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> is like light."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore: <span class="kc">immediately after <span class="h">mayim acharonim</span> — bless!</span> (<span class="h">Tichef li'netilah berachah.</span>) One must not pause at all after giving the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> its portion, lest it overpower. Flee immediately to the <span class="h">berachah</span> = binding to the <span class="h">tzadik</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">Birchas HaMazon in Any Language</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> may be said in any language. After eating properly with <span class="h">mayim rishonim</span>, <span class="h">mayim acharonim</span>, and eating in holiness — the sustenance of all the seventy nations' <span class="h">kelippos</span> and false beliefs is nullified. The <span class="h">da'as</span> is perfected; converts are made; all seventy languages return to holiness. Therefore one may bless in any language — all are now holy.</div>

<div class="p">One should also feed the poor from one's table — especially during the meal — so that converts are made and the altar/table is perfected (as in <span class="lr">LM I:17</span>). The essential <span class="h">tzedakah</span> = giving to a <span class="h">talmid chacham</span>, especially during eating: this is <span class="h">hachnasos orchim</span> [hospitality] for Torah scholars = <span class="v">"the path of <span class="h">tzadikim</span>"</span> = binding to the <span class="h">tzadik</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">Three Berachos of Birchas HaMazon</div>

<div class="p">The three <span class="h">berachos</span> of <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> correspond to the <span class="h">da'as</span> (which encompasses three: <span class="h">yirah</span> and <span class="h">ahavah</span> = <span class="h">Chesed</span> and <span class="h">Gevurah</span>, united by <span class="h">Da'as</span>). Therefore three <span class="h">berachos</span> suffice <span class="h">mi'd'Oraysa</span>. The middle <span class="h">berachah</span> (<span class="h">Birchas HaAretz</span>) = the <span class="h">tzadik</span>: <span class="v">"the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> shall inherit the land,"</span> and <span class="h">bris</span> is mentioned there = <span class="h">tzadik</span>. The first <span class="h">berachah</span> (<span class="h">HaZan</span>) = <span class="h">ahavah</span>/desire — it speaks of Hashem's goodness, kindness, mercy, and will. The third (<span class="h">Boneh Yerushalayim</span>) = <span class="h">yirah</span> = <span class="h">yirah shalem</span> [complete awe]. Through binding to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> through eating, one merits both <span class="h">yirah</span> and <span class="h">ahavah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">The Sages added a fourth <span class="h">berachah</span> — <span class="h">HaTov V'HaMeitiv</span> — to extend the <span class="h">da'as</span> into its detailed expression (four aspects in detail, though three in essence).</div>

<div class="sn">Avraham's Eshel</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"He planted an <span class="h">eshel</span> in Be'er Sheva and called there in the Name of Hashem."</span> Through eating and drinking (= <span class="h">eshel</span>, as Rashi explains), Avraham called in Hashem's Name — for through eating, <span class="h">yirah</span> and <span class="h">ahavah</span> are revealed. Avraham, the man of <span class="h">chesed</span> and <span class="h">tzedakah</span>, made converts — and through this merited the perfected altar/table.</div>

<div class="sep">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

<!-- HALACHAH 2 -->
<div class="sn" style="font-size:1.25rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);">Halachah 2</div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="lr">LM I:47</span> (<span class="v">"Va'Achaltem Achol V'Savo'a"</span>)</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Halachic framework</span>: <span class="h">Zimun</span> [invitation to bless] with three; the cup of wine for <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> — the essential cup is with three (Zohar; <span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span>).</div>

<div class="sn">Breaking the Desire for Eating = Truth = Eretz Yisrael</div>

<div class="p">From <span class="lr">LM I:47</span>: through breaking the desire for eating, one merits <span class="h">emes</span> [truth] and can perform wonders. The Sages: <span class="mx">"Shall I not show favor to Israel? I said: 'you shall eat, be satisfied, and bless' — yet they are meticulous from a <span class="h">k'zayis</span> to a <span class="h">k'beitzah</span>!"</span> Through breaking the eating-desire (= being meticulous, eating carefully) comes <span class="h">nesi'as panim</span> [showing favor] = <span class="h">emes</span>. Through this, one receives abundance from <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, which draws its sustenance from <span class="h">emes</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">Birchas HaMazon = Truth = Eretz Yisrael</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">nesi'as panim</span> = <span class="h">emes</span> = Yaakov = <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. The essential <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> is with <span class="kc">three</span> — corresponding to the three lines of <span class="h">emes</span> = the three Avos, with Yaakov encompassing all three = <span class="h">emes</span>. When three are present, the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> sitting at the table is subdued. <span class="h">Emes</span> is the opposite of the eating-desire; with three (= <span class="h">emes</span>), the desire is subdued and <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> reaches perfection: <span class="v">"you shall eat, be satisfied, and praise…"</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> is established upon <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>: <span class="v">"you shall bless Hashem your G-d for the good Land."</span> From <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, through <span class="h">emes</span>, come beautiful words of praise = <span class="h">Tiferes</span>, the totality of all colors.</div>

<div class="sn">The Cup of Birchas HaMazon</div>

<div class="p">The cup = <span class="h">Malchus</span> = <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. <span class="mx">"The cup of <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> — one must set one's eyes upon it"</span> = <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, of which it says: <span class="v">"the eyes of Hashem your G-d are constantly upon it."</span> Through <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span>, one can alter nature at will — revealing Hashem's sovereignty = the cup = <span class="h">Malchus</span>. Through <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span>, abundance flows from <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">HaTov V'HaMeitiv: Burial at Beitar</div>

<div class="p">The Sages established <span class="h">HaTov V'HaMeitiv</span> for the dead of Beitar who were given burial. This is a surprising connection to <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> — but it makes perfect sense:</div>

<div class="p">Rabbainu concludes <span class="lr">LM I:47</span> with: <span class="v">"you shall eat… and praise… and My people shall never be ashamed."</span> Through holy eating = <span class="h">emes</span>, one is saved from shame. <span class="h">Emes</span> = <span class="h">Tiferes</span>, the totality of all colors — through which one is saved from shame (= also the totality of colors, but from fallen <span class="h">middos</span>). Burial = covering the dead person's shame. <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> = <span class="h">emes</span> = salvation from shame = burial (which covers shame). Also: burial = returning souls to their source, which also happens through holy eating (elevating <span class="h">gilgulim</span>). The ultimate burial = in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>; <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> arouses <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>'s holiness through <span class="h">emes</span>. And <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> = <span class="h">emes</span> = life (<span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> = <span class="h">Eretz HaChaim</span>); burial too = life, for through it the body is purified for <span class="h">Techiyas HaMeisim</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">Zimun: Inviting the Holiness</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> requires <span class="h">zimun</span> [invitation] — inviting the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> that we are awakening. Israel has the power to create <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> wherever they are — even in <span class="h">chutz la'aretz</span>. Here, through <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> = <span class="h">emes</span>, through the cup upon which one fixes one's eyes, <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is created in this very place. Therefore we need a <span class="h">hazmanah</span> — an invitation for the new holiness we are generating.</div>

<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary — Halachos 1–2 ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>Halachah 1</strong> (based on LM I:17, I:47): Eating = drawing <em>da'as</em>. <em>Mayim rishonim</em> = drawing the <em>da'as</em>; <em>mayim acharonim</em> = sustaining the <em>da'as</em> by giving the <em>Sitra Achra</em> its measured share. Immediately after: <em>Birchas HaMazon</em> = binding to the <em>tzadik</em> who is the source of all blessings. Said in any language because all seventy nations return to holiness. Three <em>berachos</em> = <em>ahavah</em>, <em>tzadik</em>, <em>yirah</em>. Fourth = extension of <em>da'as</em>.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>Halachah 2</strong> (based on LM I:47): Breaking the eating-desire = <em>emes</em> = <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>. Three at the table = three lines of <em>emes</em> = three Avos. The cup = <em>Malchus</em> = <em>Eretz Yisrael</em> — "set your eyes upon it." <em>HaTov V'HaMeitiv</em> established for burial at Beitar because <em>Birchas HaMazon</em> = <em>emes</em> = salvation from shame = burial = life. <em>Zimun</em> = inviting the holiness of <em>Eretz Yisrael</em> being created here.</p>
</div>

